\chapter{Maps}
\label{ch:maps}

A \emph{map} is the labyrinth itself: a collection of locations
(\cref{ch:locations}) wired into a graph, with a marker for where the player
currently stands.  The Locations chapter built rooms that each know their own
doorways but deliberately left movement undone --- \enquote{Movement is the map
chapter's job} (\cref{ch:locations}).  This chapter is that job: the map
\emph{walks} the links a location only stores, and remembers the room the walk
has reached.

The contrast with a location is the whole point.  A [[Location]] is local
knowledge --- one room, its contents, the directions leading out.  A [[Map]] is
the global view --- every room of one labyrinth and, crucially, the single
\emph{current} location that turns a static graph into a player moving through
it.  Like a location it is mutable and compared by identity: the player's
position changes as they walk, and two labyrinths with identical rooms are
still two different mazes.

A map is also where the labyrinth \emph{comes to life}.  A generated map
carries the policy that furnishes a room --- fills it with things to find ---
the first time the player reaches it (\cref{sec:furnishing}); until then a
generated room stays bare.

A map aggregates locations but carries \emph{no subject}.  Which part of the
curriculum a task belongs to rides on the individual entities a room holds ---
an inscription posing a maths problem, a character asking a Swedish one ---
not on the room and not on the map (\cref{ch:entities}).  Staying
subject-agnostic this way only \emph{permits} interleaving; the furnishing step
(\cref{sec:furnishing}) is where it is actually realised.  Keeping the map
purely topological is what leaves that step free to \emph{mix} subjects along
the player's path.%
\ltnote{%
  We raise \enquote{no subject} \emph{here}, alongside the data model, rather
  than in the generator and furnishing sections that exploit it.  The reader
  meets the constraint while the [[Map]] is still fresh, so that the later
  \enquote{interleave the subjects} reads as the payoff of a decision already
  understood instead of a fact reverse-engineered from the generator.
}

Mixing subjects within one labyrinth is a deliberate choice, not an accident of
generation, and it is worth recording why.  Interleaved practice --- mixing
problem kinds rather than blocking one kind at a time --- improves retention and
the ability to choose the right method, largely because it spaces repeated
encounters with each
topic~\autocite{taylor2010interleaved,rohrer2015interleaved,cepeda2006distributed}.
A long stretch of a single subject also risks the \enquote{boredom channel},
where too little novelty disengages the
learner~\autocite{csikszentmihalyi1990flow}, whereas variety can trigger the
situational interest that grows into durable individual
interest~\autocite{hidi2006interest} --- the on-ramp for a least-favourite
subject.  The honest caveat is that the sharpest interleaving gains are measured
\emph{within} a subject (discriminating similar problem types); \emph{across}
subjects the benefit is better attributed to spacing and motivation.  Keeping
subject off the map is what makes that interleaving possible at all.

