\section{The map}

The fields are the aggregate and the marker.  [[id]] and [[name]] identify and
name the labyrinth, as for every other model.  [[locations]] holds the rooms
keyed by their [[id]] --- a mapping, not a list, because the renderer and the
saved game refer to a room by its identifier (\cref{ch:overview}) and lookup by
id is what they will need.  [[start]] is where the player enters and [[current]]
is where they are now; both begin empty and are filled as rooms are added.

Two further fields make the map more than geography.  [[populate]] is the
labyrinth's furnishing policy: a callable the maze generator binds
(\cref{sec:furnishing}) and the map applies to a room the first time the
player reaches it.  A hand-built map leaves it [[None]] and stays exactly as
authored --- the field is what distinguishes a labyrinth that furnishes itself
from one an author already furnished.  [[visited]] is the set of rooms the
player has reached; it is what makes that furnishing happen exactly once, and
the identity semantics of \cref{ch:locations} are precisely what let mutable
rooms sit in such a set.

<<classes>>=
@dataclass(eq=False, repr=False)
class Map:
    """A labyrinth of locations with a tracked current position.

    Aggregates the locations of one map (keyed by id) and remembers the
    player's current location.  Mutable and compared by identity, like the
    Location nodes it holds.  A generated map carries a ``populate`` policy
    that furnishes each room the first time the player reaches it.
    """

    id: str
    name: dict
    locations: dict = field(default_factory=dict)
    start: Location | None = None
    current: Location | None = None
    populate: Callable | None = None
    visited: set = field(default_factory=set)

    <<map methods>>
@

As with a location, we silence the generated [[repr]] and name the map by its
[[id]]: a map's fields reach into the whole graph of rooms, so the default
representation would walk the labyrinth instead of identifying it.

<<map methods>>=
def __repr__(self):
    return f"Map({self.id!r})"
@

\section{Building the labyrinth}

The maze generator (\cref{ch:overview}) creates and links locations, then hands
them to a map.  [[add_location]] registers each under its [[id]]; the
\emph{first} room added also becomes the [[start]] and the [[current]] position,
so a freshly built map always has somewhere for the player to be without a
separate \enquote{place the player} step.  The generator therefore adds the
entrance first.

<<map methods>>=
def add_location(self, location):
    """Register ``location``; the first one added becomes the start."""
    self.locations[location.id] = location
    if self.start is None:
        self.start = location
        self.current = location
@

Our tests build little maps out of linked rooms, so a pair of helpers keeps
each one short: [[_room]] makes a described location, and [[_two_room_map]]
wires two of them north--south into a map.  Because the entrance is added
first, it is the map's start and the player's current location.

<<test [[maps.py]]>>=
from maventyr.maps import *
from maventyr.locations import Location, lookup_direction
from maventyr.game import Event, Message


def _room(id, **overrides):
    fields = dict(
        name={"sv": f"rum {id}", "en": f"room {id}"},
        description={"sv": f"Rum {id}.", "en": f"Room {id}."},
    )
    fields.update(overrides)
    return Location(id=id, **fields)


def _two_room_map():
    here = _room("a")
    there = _room("b")
    here.connect("norr", "söder", there)
    karta = Map(id="karta", name={"sv": "kartan", "en": "the map"})
    karta.add_location(here)
    karta.add_location(there)
    return karta, here, there


def test_first_location_added_becomes_the_start():
    karta, here, there = _two_room_map()
    assert karta.start is here
    assert karta.current is here
@

\section{Generating the labyrinth}

The map above was wired a room at a time; a real game needs a labyrinth
conjured on demand.  [[generate_maze]] is that conjurer: given a width and a
height it returns a finished [[Map]] of that many rooms, every one reachable
from the entrance.  It is the \emph{unit} of generation --- one finite region.
A world that grows without end (\cref{ch:overview}) is built later by generating
fresh regions and joining them through the cross-map links the Locations chapter
left ready (\cref{ch:locations}); a region is finite on purpose, so that it can
be held in memory, drawn, and saved as a whole.  And like the [[Map]] it
returns, generation is purely topological: it decides the \emph{shape} of the
labyrinth and nothing about \emph{difficulty}, which rides on the tasks the
furnishing step places in the rooms (\cref{sec:furnishing}) --- the same
subject-free discipline the chapter opened with, now upheld by the generator.%
\ltnote{%
  Generation is presented here, between the manual [[add_location]] and the
  [[look]]/[[move]] operations, because it is \emph{construction}: it belongs
  beside the other way a map comes to exist, before the chapter turns to using
  one.  The reader meets every route to a finished map together, then meets the
  operations that act on any of them.
}

\subsection{Why a perfect maze, carved depth-first}

We carve the region with a \emph{randomized depth-first search}, the procedure
usually called the \enquote{recursive backtracker}.  Depth-first search is an
old idea: Trémaux used it to thread a maze in the
1880s~\autocite{lucas1882recreations}, and it was later cast as the standard
linear-time graph traversal~\autocite{tarjan1972dfs}.  The \emph{generator} runs
that same search backwards --- carving the passage it steps through rather than
reading one already there --- so the procedure that \emph{solves} a maze is the
one that \emph{builds} it.  From the entrance it walks to a random unvisited
neighbour, carving a passage as it steps, and backtracks when it paints itself
into a corner, until every cell has been visited exactly once.  Because each
cell is entered once and through exactly one carved passage, the result is a
\emph{perfect} maze: a spanning tree over the grid~\autocite{knuth2006trees},
both connected (every room is reachable) and acyclic (exactly one path between
any two rooms).
That single-path property is what suits a \emph{learning} labyrinth --- progress
is a line of rooms to be solved through, not a shortcut to be slipped around ---
and it makes the maze cheap to verify: a tree on $N$ cells has exactly $N-1$
passages, the witness the tests below lean on.

We keep an explicit stack rather than recursing, so a large region cannot
overflow the interpreter's call stack: the maze's size is a gameplay parameter,
not a quantity Python's recursion limit may cap.  And the walk's only choice ---
which neighbour to visit next --- is drawn from a seeded [[random.Random]], so a
given seed reproduces a given maze exactly.  That determinism earns its keep
twice: a saved game can store a region as its seed alone and regenerate it on
load instead of serialising every room (\cref{ch:overview}), and the tests can
assert against a fixed layout.

<<imports>>=
import random
@

\subsection{The grid as data}

A step on the grid is a change in coordinates paired with the exit it carves and
the exit that leads back.  We list the four compass moves once, as data, because
the generator is the one place that knows the grid's geometry: the Locations
chapter made [[connect]] take the return direction as an argument precisely so
that \enquote{which way is back} lives \emph{here}, with the code that knows
north is the step $(0,-1)$, rather than in a [[Location]] that knows only its own
doorways (\cref{ch:locations}).  Letting $y$ grow southward is an arbitrary but
fixed choice; a renderer is free to orient the drawing however it likes
(\cref{ch:overview}).

<<constants>>=
_STEPS = [
    (0, -1, "norr", "söder"),
    (0, 1, "söder", "norr"),
    (1, 0, "öster", "väster"),
    (-1, 0, "väster", "öster"),
]
@

Every cell starts as the same neutral room, named and described by shared
constant directories.  Keeping the rooms bare is the construction-versus-content
split made concrete: generation shapes the topology, and the furnishing step
that follows (\cref{sec:furnishing}) drops in the things that pose
the tasks.  A room is keyed by its coordinates so it has a stable handle the
carver, the renderer and the saved game can all name (\cref{ch:locations}); that
coordinate id is also the single breadcrumb of geometry the bare graph keeps,
enough for a later renderer to recover the layout without a [[Location]] having
to carry a position it does not otherwise need.

<<constants>>=
ROOM_NAME = {"sv": "en gång i labyrinten", "en": "a passage in the labyrinth"}
ROOM_DESCRIPTION = {
    "sv": "En gång i labyrinten, med väggar av grå sten.",
    "en": "A passage in the labyrinth, with walls of grey stone.",
}
MAZE_NAME = {"sv": "labyrinten", "en": "the labyrinth"}
@

<<functions>>=
def _maze_room(x, y):
    return Location(id=f"{x},{y}", name=ROOM_NAME, description=ROOM_DESCRIPTION)
