\subsection{The generator}

The function reads as its three phases: lay out the rooms, carve the passages
between them, and gather the result into a map.

<<functions>>=
def generate_maze(width, height, seed=None, id="labyrint", name=None):
    """Return a fully-connected maze Map of ``width`` by ``height`` rooms.

    The maze is carved by a seeded randomized depth-first search, so it is a
    perfect maze: every room is reachable from the start and there is exactly
    one path between any two rooms.  Passing the same ``seed`` reproduces the
    same maze.  The room at coordinates (0, 0) is the entrance, and becomes
    the map's start and current location.  Rooms start bare: each is
    furnished by the map's ``populate`` policy the first time the player
    reaches it.
    """
    <<build the grid of rooms>>
    <<carve passages with a randomized depth-first search>>
    <<assemble the rooms into a map, entrance first>>
@

We lay out every cell as an unconnected room up front, rather than creating
rooms as the walk discovers them, so the carver can look up any neighbour by
coordinate --- and so a cell the walk never \emph{starts} from is still a real
room waiting to be linked into.

<<build the grid of rooms>>=
rooms = {
    (x, y): _maze_room(x, y)
    for x in range(width)
    for y in range(height)
}
@

The walk carries a stack --- the path back to the entrance --- and a set of the
cells already visited.  At the cell on top of the stack it gathers the
neighbours still unvisited and inside the grid; finding none, it has reached a
dead end and pops back; otherwise it picks one at random, carves the two-way
passage to it, marks it visited and steps onto it.  Carving only ever \emph{into}
an unvisited cell is exactly what guarantees the tree: a passage is added once
per cell beyond the first, so no cycle can close and no cell is left unreached.

<<carve passages with a randomized depth-first search>>=
rng = random.Random(seed)
start = (0, 0)
visited = {start}
stack = [start]
while stack:
    x, y = stack[-1]
    options = [
        (dx, dy, token, back)
        for dx, dy, token, back in _STEPS
        if (x + dx, y + dy) in rooms and (x + dx, y + dy) not in visited
    ]
    if not options:
        stack.pop()
        continue
    dx, dy, token, back = rng.choice(options)
    neighbour = (x + dx, y + dy)
    rooms[(x, y)].connect(token, back, rooms[neighbour])
    visited.add(neighbour)
    stack.append(neighbour)
@

Finally the rooms become a map.  We add the entrance \emph{first} so that
[[add_location]] makes it the start and the player's position with no separate
step (\cref{ch:maps}); the rest follow in a fixed coordinate order, so that even
the [[locations]] dictionary is reproducible for a given size.  A caller may name
the region --- the distinct regions of a larger world will --- and otherwise it
takes the neutral default.

One more thing leaves the generator with the map: the name of its furnishing
policy.  The generator itself places nothing --- generation stays topological
--- but it is the only code that holds the seed, so it is here that seed and
policy must be bound together for the map to apply room by room as the player
arrives.  The chunk is defined in \cref{sec:furnishing}, beside the policy it
binds.

<<assemble the rooms into a map, entrance first>>=
labyrint = Map(id=id, name=name if name is not None else MAZE_NAME)
labyrint.add_location(rooms[start])
for coord in sorted(rooms):
    if coord != start:
        labyrint.add_location(rooms[coord])
<<name the labyrinth's populator>>
return labyrint
@

The acceptance makes three claims; we verify each.  First, the region has
exactly the rooms requested:

<<test [[maps.py]]>>=
def test_generated_maze_has_the_requested_number_of_rooms():
    maze = generate_maze(4, 5, seed=1)
    assert len(maze.locations) == 4 * 5
@

Next, \enquote{fully connected}: walking the carved passages from the entrance
reaches every room.  We follow exits with a flood fill and count what we touch:

<<test [[maps.py]]>>=
def _reachable_from(start):
    seen = set()
    stack = [start]
    while stack:
        room = stack.pop()
        if room in seen:
            continue
        seen.add(room)
        stack.extend(room.exits.values())
    return seen


def test_every_room_is_reachable_from_the_start():
    maze = generate_maze(6, 6, seed=2)
    assert len(_reachable_from(maze.start)) == len(maze.locations)
@

Reachability alone would also hold for a maze riddled with loops; that we built
a \emph{perfect} one --- a tree --- is the sharper claim, and it has a tidy
witness.  A tree on $N$ nodes has $N-1$ edges, and each two-way passage appears
as one exit at each of its ends, so the exits should total exactly $2(N-1)$:

<<test [[maps.py]]>>=
def test_the_maze_is_a_tree_with_one_path_between_rooms():
    width, height = 5, 4
    maze = generate_maze(width, height, seed=3)
    passages = sum(len(room.exits) for room in maze.locations.values())
    assert passages == 2 * (width * height - 1)
@

Determinism is the second half of the acceptance, and we pin both directions of
it.  Comparing two mazes is less direct than it looks: the Locations chapter
gave a [[Location]] \emph{identity} equality rather than value equality
(\cref{ch:locations}), so two separately generated mazes --- distinct objects
throughout --- would never test equal even when they are the same labyrinth.  We
therefore compare a \emph{structural fingerprint} instead: each room keyed by its
id, mapping every exit token to the neighbour's id.  That captures the shape the
seed determines while sidestepping object identity entirely:

<<test [[maps.py]]>>=
def _structure(maze):
    return {
        room_id: {token: dest.id for token, dest in room.exits.items()}
        for room_id, room in maze.locations.items()
    }


def test_the_same_seed_reproduces_the_same_maze():
    assert _structure(generate_maze(5, 5, seed=7)) == _structure(
        generate_maze(5, 5, seed=7)
    )


def test_different_seeds_give_different_mazes():
    assert _structure(generate_maze(5, 5, seed=7)) != _structure(
        generate_maze(5, 5, seed=8)
    )
@

The entrance is wired into the map as the Building section promised: the first
room added is both the start and the player's current location, and it is the
room at the origin:

<<test [[maps.py]]>>=
def test_the_entrance_is_the_start_and_current_room():
    maze = generate_maze(3, 3, seed=4)
    assert maze.start is maze.current
    assert maze.start is maze.locations["0,0"]
@

A degenerate one-room region is the smallest case the walk must survive: it
finds no neighbours, pops immediately, and leaves a single room with no exits
rather than failing:

<<test [[maps.py]]>>=
def test_a_single_room_maze_has_no_exits():
    maze = generate_maze(1, 1, seed=0)
    assert len(maze.locations) == 1
    assert maze.start.exits == {}
@

\section{Furnishing the rooms}
\label{sec:furnishing}

The generator left every room deliberately bare; this section is the
furnishing step the chapter has kept promising.  The obvious design ---
furnish every room as part of generation --- is the one we reject, and the
reason is the player.  The curriculum work of a later milestone will pitch a
room's content at the player's level \emph{as it is when they arrive}
(\cref{ch:overview}) --- the challenge-matching that keeps a learner in the
flow channel~\autocite{csikszentmihalyi1990flow} --- and a level read at
generation time would be stale by the time the player has walked there.  So a
room is furnished \emph{lazily}, the moment the player first reaches it.

Laziness has a consequence for saving the game that is worth fixing in print
before any code: because furnishing may one day read player state, what it
produces is \emph{materialised game state} for the persistence chapter to
record (\cref{ch:overview}).  A seed alone can regenerate the topology, but it
cannot replay what the player's own arrival created.

\subsection{Placeholder furniture}

Until the question and content chapters arrive (\cref{ch:overview}), the
furniture is subject-free flavour --- the same placeholder discipline as the
rooms' own names.  The prototypes are ordinary [[Entity]] values: frozen,
authored content exactly as the Entities chapter prescribes
(\cref{ch:entities}).  Frozen content would be perfectly safe to share ---
yet placement stamps a \emph{copy} under a room-scoped id, because the id is
the stable handle the engine and the saved game grip an entity by: two
pebbles in two rooms must answer to two names, or what the player did to one
would be remembered of both.
One prototype is takeable so that the inventory work of a later milestone
finds something liftable already in the maze.

Furnishing needs three more tools: [[Entity]] itself --- the first time this
module touches content rather than geography --- [[replace]] to stamp a
prototype into its room-scoped copy, and [[functools.partial]] to bind the
seed into the policy the map carries.

<<imports>>=
import functools
from dataclasses import replace

from maventyr.entities import Entity
@

<<constants>>=
FLAVOUR_PROTOTYPES = [
    Entity(
        id="inskrift",
        name={"sv": "en inskrift", "en": "an inscription"},
        description={
            "sv": "Någon har ristat tecken i stenväggen.",
            "en": "Someone has carved signs into the stone wall.",
        },
    ),
    Entity(
        id="fackla",
        name={"sv": "en fladdrande fackla", "en": "a flickering torch"},
        description={
            "sv": "En fackla fladdrar i sin järnhållare.",
            "en": "A torch flickers in its iron holder.",
        },
    ),
    Entity(
        id="sten",
        name={"sv": "en glittrande sten", "en": "a glittering pebble"},
        description={
            "sv": "En liten sten som glittrar i mörkret.",
            "en": "A small pebble that glitters in the dark.",
