\subsection{One random stream per room}

The policy's randomness needs more care than the generator's did.  Because
furnishing is lazy, rooms are furnished in whatever order the player explores
--- and a single random stream consumed room by room would make a room's
contents depend on how many rooms were furnished \emph{before} it, that is, on
the path the player happened to take.  The acceptance asks for the opposite:
items distributed reproducibly for a seed.  So each room draws from its
\emph{own} stream, keyed by the seed and the room's id together; a room's
contents are then a function of the room and the seed alone, the same whether
it is furnished first, last, or in a test that never walks there at all.  When
the curriculum chapter later adds the player's level, it joins that function's
\emph{arguments} --- the per-room stream needs no reshaping.

The policy itself is modest on purpose: a coin flip decides whether the room
holds anything, and a draw picks which prototype it holds.  Half the rooms
stay empty so that finding something keeps the flavour of \emph{finding}.

<<constants>>=
FLAVOUR_CHANCE = 0.5
@

<<functions>>=
def populate_room(room, seed):
    """Furnish ``room`` with placeholder flavour, reproducibly.

    Draws from a random stream keyed by ``seed`` and the room's id, so what
    a room holds is a function of the room and the seed alone, never of the
    order in which rooms are reached.  Places at most one flavour entity,
    under a room-scoped id.
    """
    rng = random.Random(f"{seed}|{room.id}")
    if rng.random() >= FLAVOUR_CHANCE:
        return
    prototype = rng.choice(FLAVOUR_PROTOTYPES)
    room.add_entity(replace(prototype, id=f"{prototype.id}@{room.id}"))
@

This is also where the generator's deferred chunk lands: it binds a furnishing
seed into the policy and hands the result to the map.  We draw that seed from
the carving stream rather than passing [[seed]] through unchanged, and the
reason is the unseeded case: [[populate_room]] keys its streams by the
\emph{value} it is given, so a literal [[None]] would furnish every unseeded
labyrinth identically --- room ids repeat across mazes --- even as their
passages differed.  The same trap is why [[populate_room]] \emph{requires}
its seed where [[generate_maze]] may omit one: there is no furnishing
stream a missing seed could sensibly mean.  One draw from [[rng]] gives
both cases the right behaviour: a seeded labyrinth determines its furniture
exactly as it determines its passages, and an unseeded one stays
unpredictable throughout.

<<name the labyrinth's populator>>=
labyrint.populate = functools.partial(populate_room, seed=rng.random())
@

Reproducibility is the acceptance, so the tests fingerprint a furnished maze
--- each room's id mapped to the sorted ids of what it holds --- and check the
fingerprint is a function of the seed: the same seed gives the same furniture
even when the rooms are done in the opposite order, and a different seed gives
different furniture.  The last test pins the \enquote{distributed} in
\enquote{distributed along paths}: some rooms hold something, some hold
nothing.

<<test [[maps.py]]>>=
def _furnish(maze, seed, reverse=False):
    rooms = list(maze.locations.values())
    for room in reversed(rooms) if reverse else rooms:
        populate_room(room, seed)
    return {
        room.id: sorted(entity.id for entity in room.entities)
        for room in maze.locations.values()
    }


def test_furnishing_is_reproducible_for_a_seed():
    assert _furnish(generate_maze(5, 5, seed=11), 11) == _furnish(
        generate_maze(5, 5, seed=11), 11
    )


def test_the_order_of_furnishing_does_not_matter():
    assert _furnish(generate_maze(5, 5, seed=11), 11) == _furnish(
        generate_maze(5, 5, seed=11), 11, reverse=True
    )


def test_a_different_seed_furnishes_differently():
    assert _furnish(generate_maze(5, 5, seed=11), 11) != _furnish(
        generate_maze(5, 5, seed=11), 12
    )


def test_furniture_lands_in_some_rooms_but_not_all():
    furnished = _furnish(generate_maze(5, 5, seed=11), 11)
    assert any(furnished.values())
    assert not all(furnished.values())
@

\subsection{Arrival is the trigger}

What remains is the moment itself: \emph{when} does a room get furnished?
[[discover]] is the map's answer --- on the player's first arrival, and never
again.  The method lives on the map because the map owns both halves of the
decision: the memory ([[visited]]) that says whether this arrival is the
first, and the policy ([[populate]]) that says what a first arrival creates.
The [[visited]] check is what turns \enquote{furnished lazily} into
\enquote{furnished exactly once} --- looking around twice must not stack two
inscriptions on the same wall --- and the [[None]] guard is the authored-map
case from the chapter's opening: a map built by hand has no policy and is
never altered by being walked.

<<map methods>>=
def discover(self, location):
    """Mark ``location`` reached, furnishing it on the first arrival.

    Applies the map's ``populate`` policy (if any) the first time the
    player reaches ``location``; later arrivals change nothing.
    """
    if location in self.visited:
        return
    self.visited.add(location)
    if self.populate is not None:
        self.populate(location)
@

Nothing calls [[discover]] yet --- the perception operations of the next two
sections do, which is what makes arrival mean \enquote{seen}, not merely
\enquote{stored in [[current]]}.  The tests therefore drive it the way the
player will: a fresh maze is bare everywhere, looking around furnishes the
entrance once and only once, walking into a room furnishes it once and only
once, and a hand-built map comes through both untouched.

<<test [[maps.py]]>>=
def test_a_fresh_maze_is_bare_until_reached():
    maze = generate_maze(3, 3, seed=5)
    assert all(room.entities == [] for room in maze.locations.values())


def test_looking_furnishes_the_entrance_exactly_once():
    maze = generate_maze(3, 3, seed=5)
    maze.look()
    furniture = list(maze.start.entities)
    assert furniture
    maze.look()
    assert maze.start.entities == furniture


def test_entering_a_room_furnishes_it_exactly_once():
    maze = generate_maze(2, 1, seed=5)
    maze.move("öster")
    entered = maze.current
    furniture = list(entered.entities)
    maze.move("väster")
    maze.move("öster")
    assert entered.entities == furniture


def test_a_hand_built_map_stays_as_authored():
    karta, here, there = _two_room_map()
    karta.look()
    karta.move("norr")
    assert here.entities == [] and there.entities == []
@

\section{Looking around}

A map shows the player their surroundings in two steps.  First it
\emph{discovers} the current room --- the trigger the furnishing section
prepared (\cref{sec:furnishing}), so that a first glance is what makes a
generated room's contents real.  Then it delegates the describing to the
location itself: the map knows \emph{where} the player is, the location knows
what that place looks like (\cref{ch:locations}), and [[look]] adds no wording
of its own, keeping a room's description defined in exactly one place.
Hanging discovery on this one method is no accident: the engine funnels every
look --- the opening description of a new game included --- through it
(\cref{ch:game}), so perception and discovery cannot drift apart, with no
second hook anywhere.

<<map methods>>=
def look(self, language=DEFAULT_LANGUAGE):
    """Return events describing the player's current location.

    Looking discovers the room: a first visit furnishes it via the map's
    ``populate`` policy before it is described.
    """
    self.discover(self.current)
    return self.current.look(language)
@

<<test [[maps.py]]>>=
def test_look_describes_the_current_location():
    karta, here, there = _two_room_map()
    assert karta.look("sv") == here.look("sv")
@

\section{Walking the links}

Moving is the chapter's reason to exist.  The player names a direction in their
own words --- \enquote{norr}, or \enquote{north} in English --- so [[move]]
first resolves that word to the token an exit is keyed by (\cref{ch:locations}),
which is what lets an English player walk a maze whose exits were carved with
Swedish tokens.  It then asks the current location where that token leads; a
missing exit is a wall to report, not an error to raise, so the map answers with
a single message and leaves the player where they were.  Otherwise it makes the
room reached the new [[current]] and returns the move's announcement followed by
the map's own [[look]] --- arriving and seeing the new place in one turn, as in
the games this one descends from.  Routing the arrival through [[self.look]]
rather than the destination's own makes entering a room \emph{perceive} it:
the discovery that furnishes a first visit (\cref{sec:furnishing}) fires on
walking in exactly as on looking around.  The resolved token, not the raw
word, then flows on to the announcement, so the message phrases the move from
the registered [[Direction]] rather than whatever the player happened to type.

<<map methods>>=
def move(self, direction, language=DEFAULT_LANGUAGE):
    """Walk the exit ``direction`` from the current location.

    ``direction`` is the word the player typed, in their language; it is
    resolved to a canonical exit token before the exit is looked up.  Returns
    a message announcing the move followed by the entered room's ``look``; or
    a single message when there is no exit that way, leaving the current
    location unchanged.  Entering discovers the room, like looking does.
    """
    token = resolve_direction(direction, language)
    destination = self.current.neighbor(token)
    if destination is None:
        return [Message(localize(NO_EXIT, language))]
    self.current = destination
    return [
        self.movement_message(token, language),
        *self.look(language),
    ]
@

<<constants>>=
NO_EXIT = {
    "sv": "Du kan inte gå dit.",
    "en": "You can't go that way.",
}
@

The announcement is where a [[Direction]]'s two forms earn their keep.  A
direction with an adverb reads naturally as the act of moving --- \enquote{Du
går norrut.} --- so we use it whenever one is present.  A named exit without an
adverb (the cross-map \enquote{dörr}, before anyone gives it one) would mangle
that sentence, so it falls back to heading \emph{towards} the exit's name.  An
author who wants natural phrasing for such an exit need only give its
[[Direction]] an adverb (say \enquote{in i portalen}), and it flows through the
first template instead --- the open registry (\cref{ch:locations}) is the seam
that makes this an authoring choice rather than a code change.

<<map methods>>=
def movement_message(self, direction, language=DEFAULT_LANGUAGE):
    """Return the ``Message`` announcing travel in ``direction``."""
    way = lookup_direction(direction)
    adverb = way.adverb(language)
    if adverb is not None:
        return Message(localize(MOVE, language).format(adverb=adverb))
    return Message(localize(TOWARDS, language).format(name=way.name(language)))
@

<<constants>>=
MOVE = {
    "sv": "Du går {adverb}.",
    "en": "You go {adverb}.",
}
TOWARDS = {
    "sv": "Du går mot {name}.",
    "en": "You head for {name}.",
}
@

A step north updates the current location and returns the adverb-shaped
announcement ahead of the new room's own description, in the player's language:

<<test [[maps.py]]>>=
def test_moving_updates_current_and_appends_the_new_room_look():
    karta, here, there = _two_room_map()
    events = karta.move("norr", "sv")
    assert karta.current is there
    assert events[0] == Message("Du går norrut.")
    assert events[1:] == there.look("sv")


def test_movement_announcement_is_localized():
    karta, here, there = _two_room_map()
    assert karta.move("norr", "en")[0] == Message("You go north.")
@

And the resolution earns its keep: a player running the game in English walks
the same Swedish-carved maze by typing an English direction word, reaching the
room the Swedish token leads to:

<<test [[maps.py]]>>=
def test_an_english_direction_word_walks_the_swedish_maze():
    karta, here, there = _two_room_map()
    events = karta.move("north", "en")
    assert karta.current is there
    assert events[0] == Message("You go north.")
    assert events[1:] == there.look("en")
@

Bumping a wall --- a direction with no exit --- reports it in one line and does
not move the player:

<<test [[maps.py]]>>=
def test_bumping_a_wall_reports_it_and_stays_put():
    karta, here, there = _two_room_map()
    events = karta.move("väster", "sv")
    assert events == [Message("Du kan inte gå dit.")]
    assert karta.current is here
@

A named exit with no adverb takes the \enquote{towards} phrasing rather than a
mangled sentence, while still moving the player through it:

<<test [[maps.py]]>>=
def test_a_named_exit_without_an_adverb_heads_towards_it():
    here = _room("a")
    annex = _room("b")
    here.link("dörr", annex)
    karta = Map(id="k", name={"sv": "k", "en": "k"})
    karta.add_location(here)
    karta.add_location(annex)
    assert karta.move("dörr", "sv")[0] == Message("Du går mot dörr.")
    assert karta.current is annex
@

[[look]] tracks the player: after a move it describes the room just entered.
And whatever happens, [[move]] honours the core's contract --- it returns a list
of [[Event]]s, and an unknown language falls back to the default rather than
failing:

<<test [[maps.py]]>>=
def test_look_follows_the_player_after_a_move():
    karta, here, there = _two_room_map()
    karta.move("norr", "sv")
    assert karta.look("sv") == there.look("sv")


def test_move_returns_a_list_of_events():
    karta, here, there = _two_room_map()
    events = karta.move("norr")
    assert isinstance(events, list)
    assert all(isinstance(event, Event) for event in events)


def test_move_falls_back_to_the_default_language():
    moved = _two_room_map()[0].move("norr", "xx")
    default = _two_room_map()[0].move("norr", DEFAULT_LANGUAGE)
    assert moved == default
@

Finally the cross-map claim from the Locations chapter, now walked: a link can
lead into a room held by a \emph{different} map, and moving along it simply makes
that foreign room the current one.  No special machinery is needed --- the exit
is an object reference like any other (\cref{ch:locations}); \emph{which} map the
player is then \enquote{in} is a question for the loop that owns the player
(\cref{ch:overview}), not for the map walking the link.

<<test [[maps.py]]>>=
def test_walking_a_cross_map_link_enters_the_other_maps_room():
    cave = _room("grotta")
    hall = _room("sal")
    cave.link("dörr", hall)
    karta_a = Map(id="a", name={"sv": "a", "en": "a"})
    karta_a.add_location(cave)
    karta_b = Map(id="b", name={"sv": "b", "en": "b"})
    karta_b.add_location(hall)
    karta_a.move("dörr")
    assert karta_a.current is hall
    assert hall in karta_b.locations.values()
@
